# An unbound rule conclusion
#
# A rule fires when Alice knows Bob, but its conclusion mentions a variable,
# ?someone, that no premise binds. Such a rule cannot name anyone in
# particular, so Eyeron derives no new fact: the output is empty.

@prefix : <https://eyereasoner.github.io/eyepl/examples/check-unsafe#> .

:alice :knows :bob .

# The premise holds, but ?someone appears only in the conclusion. To say that
# some individual is seen, the conclusion would use a blank node instead.
{
    :alice :knows :bob
} => {
    ?someone a :Seen
} .
